{
 "verdict": {
  "verdict": "No meaningful gain: clinical detail (stage, comorbidities) adds nothing even inside Synthea; Synthea-derived scores give the MEPS model at most a tiny Brier gain (about -0.001) and no clear AUROC gain",
  "detail": "(1) Inside Synthea (660 synthetic patients, 4033 person-years), clinical-only features (stage, metastasis, comorbidities, opioids, kidney function) add no signal over shared variables: AUROC 0.671 -> 0.670, d -0.001 [-0.008, +0.007]; stage is missing in 86% of person-years. (2) A Synthea model on shared variables only, applied unchanged to real MEPS survivors, ranks them at AUROC 0.686 [0.634, 0.740] with O/E 1.02: the shared risk pattern (prior acute care, age, insurance) transports. (3) Added to the MEPS model: clinical-burden score d AUROC +0.004 [-0.002, +0.011], d Brier -0.0012 [-0.0022, -0.0002]; Synthea prior d AUROC +0.004 [-0.003, +0.010], d Brier -0.0009 [-0.0018, +0.0001]; stratum features (3 definitions) d AUROC between -0.006 and -0.003. Without a shared key every Synthea feature is a function of variables MEPS already has, so a gain means the simulation is a useful prior at n=466, not that real stage or comorbidity data would help these patients. The submission uses the MEPS-only model."
 },
 "design": "Do the synthetic clinical records (Synthea) add anything beyond the MEPS survey?\n\nUsage: .venv/bin/python -I analysis/synthea.py [data]     (expects data/meps and data/synthea; run train.py first)\nWrites analysis/out/synthea.json.\n\nThere is no key between MEPS and Synthea, so no individual can be linked. Three pre-registered questions:\n  Q1 (inside Synthea): do clinical-only features (stage, comorbidities, metastasis, opioids, kidney function)\n     add AUROC over the features both sources share? Person-years, CV grouped by patient.\n     Pass: paired dAUROC 95% CI above 0.\n  Q2 (transport): a Synthea model trained on shared features only, applied unchanged to MEPS dev survivors.\n     Pass: AUROC CI above 0.5. Also reports calibration (O/E) to show the population shift.\n  Q3 (increment in MEPS): add Synthea-derived features to the MEPS model, same repeated CV as train.py,\n     paired against MEPS-only. (a) transferred \"clinical burden\" score = E[clinical part of the Synthea logit |\n     shared features]; (b) shrunken stratum features (acute rate, advanced-stage share, comorbidity burden)\n     under three stratum definitions. Pass: dAUROC CI above 0 or dBrier CI below 0.\nOnly MEPS survivors with breast, colon, lung or prostate cancer are aligned to strata (Synthea has only those);\neveryone else gets the overall Synthea mean plus an \"unaligned\" flag.",
